#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105f4c4e-d4f2-5528-bfdd-59865716d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105f4c4e-d4f2-5528-bfdd-59865716d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218} :answer-field/id #uuid "5551b954-fda2-5f2d-abc7-527dd2646792" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f6ac78e-cb74-5768-92ef-290d5885de7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343459" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "5abd7c36-14a4-56ef-ada9-6c206b80a9d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230295}] :question/id #uuid "968f331f-040e-5a16-a459-23a91c08dfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343473" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5kg$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force\n$F(t) = (3\\cos^{2} (2t) - \\frac{3}{2})N$\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}} $kg/s^{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cca9f77e-1eac-5dd3-b2b8-a6a15bf83357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cca9f77e-1eac-5dd3-b2b8-a6a15bf83357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fa88ca7-4486-52c4-a8ca-dfdd856acf42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7756f2f0-f8ff-5a38-a573-589b8cc562e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343691" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000aed7e-35ac-576a-a3d7-c9129bf6c269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315111} {:answer/id #uuid "07e35f55-7ae9-5367-82ea-35beb3942aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315159} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab66cc1-84a5-535c-955c-40c0c5cdd540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315175} {:answer/id #uuid "7a12bc81-b21e-5b8b-8236-ad2fea39d749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315798} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a4e701-0c0f-5103-bfb3-3e5526af6267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "000aed7e-35ac-576a-a3d7-c9129bf6c269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315111} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f04f5ca-c103-52cd-8991-e52037705a05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cf1f6c6-b17b-5c90-a172-f49c17abaa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343746" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7998eb-7eaf-5245-8c62-e37c7ec23280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427247} {:answer/id #uuid "25a494c8-57d1-53c2-87ed-c49b33af7999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427248} {:answer/id #uuid "59b394d0-eddc-5f9f-8b4a-e200803eeb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427255} {:answer/id #uuid "85e3b9f0-cb0f-56b7-9950-1757837f3c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427257} {:answer/id #uuid "95e627ca-5870-5885-9e85-231290fb62f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e3b9f0-cb0f-56b7-9950-1757837f3c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427257} :answer-field/id #uuid "381db720-afce-51ba-af73-b0e9b580a593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "528870e0-56ba-55b5-a9bc-4fec3b9615fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343753" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solution III matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
